[Music]  we're now reaching the end of the fourth  year of the worlds of speculative  fiction series for those of you who  don't know what that is  it's a talk series that I do here  locally partnering with the Brookfield  Public Library in the Greater Milwaukee  area and each month I take an author and  some set of their works that have a  coherent narrative universe and I come  in and give a little bit of background  on them talk about their world building  and then bring up some philosophical  themes over the years we've actually  gotten quite a few regulars who now very  much enjoy coming in and reading and  talking about these things sometimes  they actually know a little bit more  than I do and we're heading into year 5  so we've also got a tradition where I  get suggestions from all of you out  there in the ether on the internet  worldwide about who we ought to do next  so that's what this video is about and  you will be able to comment and make  some suggestions about who we ought to  do before you you launch into that I  want to tell you about who we did this  last year and who we've done in the  years past and then who we already have  coming up for 2020 so that you're not  just you know throwing spitballs out  there that have already gone stale you  might say all right so let's talk about  who we actually have lined up this  coming year  I have authors all the way from January  to September so that's nine and I'm  looking for three more slots to be  filled two of the ones that I have are  actually suggestions that came to me  from some of the regulars who attend the  series here locally and then three will  be ones that you get to suggest and then  ultimately vote on so what are they in  January we're doing Mary Shelley will be  looking at Frankenstein and the last man  I didn't get to do Shelley this this  last year so this is sort of a repeat in  February  we're doing Chyna Melville's boss-like  novels so that's perdido Street Station  the scar and iron Council in March will  be doing Stephen Donaldson's Chronicles  of Thomas covenant we're gonna look at  the first three books although I will  have by that time read all of the rest  of them as well that's quite an  investment in the series then we do  leave C's shins remembrance of Earth's  past series the three-body problem the  dark forest and deaths end in May we're  gonna do Veronica Roth's carved the mark  universe so the carve the mark book  fates divided and then some of the  stories from the end of the end and  other beginnings you may remember I did  a conversation with Veronica Roth here  in Milwaukee about her new book the end  in the other beginnings just about a  month and a half ago so I actually asked  her well should we do it on divergent  and that universe or should we do it on  carve the mark and she said that carve  the mark is probably narrative ly richer  and that was my intuition already so I  took that confirmation and ran with it  then in June we're gonna be talking  about somebody from the Milwaukee area  who went by the moniker cordwainer Smith  we'll be looking at his instrumentality  of humanity universe which includes a  lot of great short stories one of which  I read when I was very young and fell in  love with then then in July will be  since it's quite timely looking at  Margaret Atwood's Gilead novels so  that's the long ago written Handmaid's  Tale and then her recent book the  Testaments in August  great suggestion from one of the regular  attendees Larry Nivens Ringworld series  there's four books in that series all of  which are you know Ringworld this  Ringworld that and then in September  we're going to be looking at Marion  Zimmer Bradley as darkover series there  are a plethora of books in that series  so I picked storm Queen Hawke mistress 2  to conquer  and the heirs of Hammerfell because it's  right there in the middle of the you  know warring nations period of her her  narrative arc and universe and timeline  so there's three spots left over and  you're shortly going to get to make some  suggestions about those but first we  need to talk very briefly about all the  other authors that we've covered already  in this so I'm just gonna give you the  list here's the the authors that we've  already done so don't suggest these  because we've already done them Edgar  Allen Poe  GK Chesterton Lewis Carroll Tanith Lee  Gordon Dixon August Derleth Carl Edward  Wagner annals Huxley Bram Stoker and our  Scott backer the others that we  discussed in previous years would  include j.r.r tolkien a van Voigt CS  Lewis  Isaac Asimov Frank Herbert Roger Zelazny  Ursula Kayla Greene Michael Moorcock  philip k dick Mervyn Peake george RR  martin Philip Jose farmer Madeleine  L'Engle Douglas Adams Anne McCaffrey  Orson Scott Card Ian banks HP Lovecraft  William Gibson CL Moore  Octavia Butler horny Louie bore Hays  Fritz Leiber Robert Heinlein L Sprague  de camp Andre Norton Arthur Clarke  Robert Howard Gene Wolfe  CJ Terra and Jack Vance so that's those  are the ones that we're not going to  revisit and I'm going to put a list of  all of this within the video description  so just click on the you know read more  and you can see the whole list I am  however looking for suggestions what I'm  going to do is take the suggestions that  people leave as comments on this video  or on my social media in the post  specifically about this and I will take  the top say six or seven suggested  people so we're looking for those who  get to  Juston more than once by more than one  person right and don't you know don't  try to game creating a whole bunch of  fake personas to do this but we're gonna  take the top six or seven and then we'll  have a vote in say about a week or so  from now and then we'll have the lineup  for the final three thinkers for October  November December of the twenty twenty  year five of the worlds of speculative  fiction series so if there is an author  that isn't on that list of people that  we've already covered or people that  we're already going to cover and they  have a narrative universe not just one  book but but spread out across a couple  stories and coherent place doesn't even  have to be necessarily coherent  characters but a coherent place in which  it's set that's the sort of thing that  we're looking for speculative fiction so  you know we're not looking for a  realistic fiction we're looking for all  the different genres that fall under  that like fantasy horror sci-fi  alternate history utopian dystopian  literature you can look up what  speculative fiction is there's a lot of  discussion about that so I hope that  you'll get in on this and give me some  good suggestions and that you'll also  join in on the big float when we do that  [Music]

